  ('5232323', 'Walter F. George Lake Hunting Permits', 'US Army Corps of Engineers', 'Georgia', 'Fort Gaines', 'The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers at Walter F George Lake Project issues on average, annually, five hundred hunting permits on a first-come, first-serve basis for eight designated management areas. The permits are subject to a $20.00 special activity fee that is assessed each season. Walter F George Lake Project Hunting Permits include permit number, name, pertinent State hunting license number. Hunting area maps and a copy of current hunting regulations may be viewed or obtained from the  Walter F George website.    
 The Special Activities Permit Fee is assessed to cover the cost to manage th', '31.63491400000000', '-85.04697600000000', true, 'https://www.recreation.gov/permits/5232323', 'https://cdn.recreation.gov/public/2024/05/21/18/52/5232323_a9dbab2d-8055-4059-917c-2eb327ab045d_700.webp', now()),
  ('5121212', 'Blackwater National Wildlife Refuge Deer Hunt Permits', 'Fish and Wildlife Service', 'Maryland', 'Cambridge', 'Blackwater National Wildlife Refuge (NWR), located 12 miles south of Cambridge, Maryland, was established in 1933 as a refuge for migratory waterfowl. The refuge includes more than 34,000 acres, composed mainly of rich tidal marsh characterized by fluctuating water levels and variable salinity. Other habitats include freshwater ponds, mixed evergreen and deciduous forests, cropland and managed impoundments that are seasonally flooded for waterfowl.   Blackwater NWR is home to the largest natural population of formerly endangered Delmarva Peninsula fox squirrels and is also home to one of the l', '38.44652300000000', '-76.10609700000001', true, 'https://www.recreation.gov/permits/5121212', 'https://cdn.recreation.gov/public/2026/05/27/17/04/5121212_3ef207a7-fe5a-4d8d-b4e8-5f5a59368799_700.jpg', now()),
  ('5141414', 'Blackwater National Wildlife Refuge Turkey Hunt Lottery and Permits', 'Fish and Wildlife Service', 'Maryland', 'Cambridge', 'Blackwater National Wildlife Refuge (NWR), located 12 miles south of Cambridge, Maryland, was established in 1933 as a refuge for migratory waterfowl. The refuge includes more than 33,000 acres, composed mainly of rich tidal marsh characterized by fluctuating water levels and variable salinity.  Other habitats include freshwater ponds, mixed evergreen and deciduous forests, cropland and managed impoundments that are seasonally flooded for waterfowl.  
